{"qid":"Q31","names":["Kingdom of Belgium"],"title":"Kingdom of Belgium","endonym":{"native":"Königreich Belgien","lang":"de"},"capsule":"The Kingdom of Belgium emerged from the Belgian Revolution of 1830, when the southern provinces of the United Kingdom of the Netherlands revolted; the London Conference of 1830–31 recognised Belgian independence and established a constitutional monarchy, with Leopold I of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld enthroned in July 1831. Belgium industrialised rapidly in the nineteenth century, becoming the first continental European country to experience the Industrial Revolution. King Leopold II acquired the Congo Free State as a personal possession in 1885, and his brutal rubber extraction regime there became an international scandal that forced transfer of the colony to the Belgian state in 1908. Belgium has operated as a federal state since 1993, balancing Flemish, Walloon, and Brussels-Capital communities.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Belgium's metropolitan borders are treaty-defined and precisely known; the dataset reflects internationally recognised boundaries, with the caveat that Eupen-Malmedy was acquired from Germany only in 1920.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Belgium","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgium"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Belgian Revolution","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Revolution"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Leopold I of Belgium","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leopold_I_of_Belgium"}],"britannica":"place/Belgium","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Belgium","rulers":[{"qid":"Q289570","name":"Etienne Constantin de Gerlache","start":1831